From dc73cf2ab0ac8244cdbbc1e937ac4cee363345ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 17:44:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08122/11362] [outer-loop] The engine-store overwrite is
 half-fixed: within-call retention is delivered, cross-call is live but
 near-inert -- and it cannot block planning on its own

Verified rather than assumed. Within-call retention (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6035, commits
11cd3c3a9/1475245) is SHIPPED and DELIVERED -- confirmed by running the real
execute_bounded_llm_reinduction and reading the store bytes back, with a
retention-OFF control proving the axis moves.

Cross-call is a different defect and no committed REQ covers it: best_signal is
re-initialised to -inf every call, so the incumbent on disk is never a candidate.
Demonstrated: call 1 retains a 0.857-signal engine, call 2 replaces it with a
0.0-signal one and reports restored=true while doing it. The live single-shot
path (arc_competition_agent.py:6497->6506) has no retention at all.

But it does NOT independently block planning. e3.load_engine has four references
in the live agent, all in _induce_and_plan, all preceded by a proposer write in
the same call; the instrumented runs recorded 11 reads and 0 read-before-write.
Nothing reads the incumbent, so the overwrite removes an option nothing exercises
-- a store fix alone would be inert by construction. It is therefore NOT an
alternative explanation for 0-of-136. The trust gate is not made stale either:
the gate always scores bytes written microseconds earlier in the same round.

Exposure measured before claiming effect: 11 of 72 real cells (15.3%) induce
twice at all, nine of twelve games never do, and 1 of 7 eligible cells shows a
regressive write (vc33, 1.0 -> 0.932). No p-value is claimed anywhere -- the one
live endpoint that could carry a test was already run and is uninterpretable
(8 of 12 pairs byte-identical, min reachable p = 1.0).

Also corrects the framing that p=4.9e-5 measured retention's live worth: that p
is an offline counterfactual over the store's git history, not a behavioural A/B.

